## 1. Introduction
## 1.1 Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)
Remarkable progress regarding porous materials has been achieved owing to the developments in functional nanoporous materials [1-6], including metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [7-10]. MOFs are typical hybrid inorganic-organic framework materials and are made of two major components: a metal ion or a cluster of metal ions and an organic molecule (called a linker), which is coordinated to the metallic component. The organic units are typically di-, tri-, or tetradentate ligands such as BDC (benzenedicarboxylate) and BTC (benzenetricarboxylate) [7-10]. Figure 1 shows a schematic drawing of the formation of a well-known MOF, MOF-5 (zinc-BDC, $Z{\mathrm{n}}_{4}{\mathrm{O}}({\mathrm{BDC}})_{3},$ , by combining a $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ cluster and BDC [11]. The formed structure can be extended infinitely in all three dimensions, leading to a porous framework structure after adequate activation. Some typical MOFs are shown in Figure 2 [12-14]. The chemical compositions of Cu-BTC, MIL-100 and CPO-27 are $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{3}]}_{n},$ $\mathrm{Cr_{3}F(H_{2}O)_{3}O[B T C]_{2}}{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ and $\mathrm{[Co_{2}(C_{8}H_{2}O_{6})(H_{2}O)_{2}]\cdot8H_{2}O}$ respectively [12-14]. In this review, the MOFs themselves will not be discussed in detail, because there are many excellent review articles on MOFs and their applications [15-28].
Various MOFs have generally been synthesized by slow diffusion techniques, conventional hydrothermal and solvothermal methods [7-10,29]. However, the reactions used to synthesize many MOFs, particularly those with good thermal stability, require hydro/solvothermal synthesis conditions. Since hydro/solvothermal reactions with conventional heating methods typically require up to several days, alternative techniques such as MW and US irradiation have been introduced to develop facile, rapid, inexpensive, and commercially viable routes toward the production of these compounds to facilitate their use in various applications [30,31]. Due to rapid/homogeneous heating and fast kinetics, the
MW-assisted hydrothermal method is applied successfully for decreasing reaction time of MOFs syntheses up to a few minutes with high phase purity and high yield [32-41]. Moreover, US synthesis method has attracted growing attention in regard to the synthesis of various materials. This method is an efficient way to synthesize MOFs, especially nanoparticles, with a short crystallization time and especially with low reaction temperature [42-48].
## 1.2 Microwave in chemistry
In chemistry, conventional heating methods mainly include the electric heating, sand baths, oil baths, and heating jackets. Many attempts have been made during the last decade to introduce alternative energy sources by considering working efficiency, environmental friendliness, and energy consumption. MW consists of an electromagnetic radiation, which lies between radio waves and infrared frequencies, with relatively large wavelengths of $1\mathrm{mm}$ to 1 m [49,50]. Dipole rotation and ionic conduction are the two fundamental mechanisms for transferring energy from MWs to the substance being heated [51,52]. As shown in Figure 3, MW heating normally can be carried out through the interaction of electromagnetic radiation with the dipole moment of the molecules, whereas dipole moments rotate to align themselves with the alternating electric field of the MW [31,50-54]. Therefore, water or ionic liquids with a high dipole moment are considered to be the best solvents for use in MW-based syntheses [31,51,55,56]. MW irradiation has been used for heating food materials and is now employed for a variety of chemical applications including organic synthesis [31,49-51,57- 61]. MW-assisted synthesis is a relatively new chemical method that facilitates the green syntheses of nanomaterials [54,55,62-64] including nanoporous materials [65-69]. Recently, the MW technique has also been successfully employed in the synthesis of organic-inorganic hybrid materials including MOFs [30,31,70-89], and therefore, plays a crucial role in their applications. MW heating leads several advantages to the synthesis of various materials.
## 1.3 Ultrasound in chemistry
US irradiation is one of the nonconventional techniques that has proved to be superior in terms of operation under ambient conditions, operational simplicity, product selectivity, and reduced reaction times. Recently, the use of US irradiation in chemistry, i.e., sonochemistry, has grown significantly, resulting in a considerable part of empirical research. In this technique, the reactant molecules undergo chemical reactions due to the application of powerful US waves (20 KHz - 1 MHz) [49]. The sonochemical process in liquids is based on the generation, growth, and collapse of bubbles, also known as acoustic cavitation [90-96], which results in extreme local heating, high pressures, and very short lifetimes (Figure 4). These transient and localized hot spots obtained by cavitation have temperatures of ${\sim}5000\mathrm{K}$ , pressures of ${\sim}1000$ atmospheres, and heating and cooling rates above $10^{10}\mathrm{K/s}$ [90-96]. This exceptional physical phenomenon serves as a means of concentrating the diffuse energy of sound into a unique set of “super conditions” to produce unusual materials from precursors dissolved in solution through high-energy chemical reactions [96]. As a result, a number of chemical reactions that were previously dicult to carry out by other heating methods can easily be accomplished using sonication. Very recently, the facile synthesis [97-112], purification [113,114], and modification [115] of MOF-based porous materials with US were reported and highlighted the unique and superior properties of US.
## 1.4 Accelerated synthesis with MW/US
Porous materials are typically synthesized via batch processes due to long reaction times, thus preventing continuous production [116]. MW irradiation will directly trigger molecules that possess a dipole, and therefore the energy transfer occurs in less than a nanosecond and, results in a high instantaneous temperature $\mathrm{(T_{i})}$ of the molecules. The instantaneous temperature is a function of MW power input, and greater MW power intensities will result in a higher and more consistent $\mathrm{T_{i}}$ [52].  MW-assisted reaction rates can be faster than those of conventional heating methods by as much as 1,000-fold [52]; in the majority of cases, the reason for the rate enhancement is a thermal/kinetic effect [61]. Based on experimental data from numerous studies, chemists have proposed an explanation for expedited synthesis via MW, including, (i) an increase in the heating rate of the reaction mixture, (ii) more uniform heating of the reaction mixture, (iii) change in the association between species within the mixture, (iv) superheating of the mixture, (v) creation of hot spots, and (vi) enhancement of the dissolution of the precursor gel [31,65,66,117-119]. According to Conner et al. [118], rapid heating and the creation of hot spots are important factors associated with increased reaction rates under MW. MW-assisted syntheses of various materials such as metals, metal oxides, inorganic hybrid materials, MOF materials, etc., have been reported elsewhere [30,31,62-65,118,120,121]. MW-assisted syntheses may be fascinating because of high product yield in some cases in the syntheses of materials including MOFs, metal phosphates and metal oxides [70,75,76,79,119,122-136]. More importantly, due to the improved physiochemical properties (because of mainly small size), the porous materials synthesized by MW heating showed excellent performances in various adsorption processes [34,42,73,77,78,137,138,139].
Various accelerated syntheses in organic reactions with US have been reported [51,95,140,141]. The high/intense US wave can not only lead to homogeneous nucleation but also accelerates chemical reactions and initiates new reactions that are difficult to achieve under normal conditions [91,141]. In sonochemical reactions, the chemical reactivity increases with the production of heat, promotion of mixing (stirring) or mass transfer,
promotion of intimate contact between materials, and production of free radicals [95]. It has been observed that the cavitation power of US reduces the reaction temperature, and higher reaction rates can be achieved under ambient conditions [90-93]. Recently, US was successfully used in the field of materials chemistry in order to reduce the synthesis time [30,55,93,94,142-144], to get excellent product yield in some cases   
[43,45,47,98,100,102,106,129,145-147] and to improve adsorption performances   
[42,99,102,105,129,138,146,148].
## 1.5 Phase-selective synthesis with MW/US
The synthesis of materials with different structures, chemical compositions and physical properties from the same reaction component has been reported elsewhere [123,124,130,131,149-156]. Different products can be obtained by changing external reaction parameters such as reaction time, temperature etc. [123,124,130,131,149-156]. The selective synthesis of a particular phase of products with specific and distinctive crystal structure (less stable or hard to synthesize) from the same reaction components can be thought of as phaseselective synthesis. The heating method may be an alternative parameter for the phaseselective synthesis of some materials, even though it is not very common [123,124,130,131,149-156]. So far, MW irradiation has been reported in the synthesis of some specific phases of crystals, while conventional heating would produce different or other phases under the same reaction conditions [123,124,130,131,149,150,153,155,156]. Since MW irradiation leads to extremely fast crystallization, it is likely that a part of the material (kinetically favorable phase, less stable phase) crystallizes with MW heating, but is especially hard to synthesize with conventional heating due to the interconversion of the less stable phase into the more stable or dense phases. The phase-selective synthesis of less stable structures via MW leads to a new method to synthesize a phase, which even has not been
observed by conventional synthesis (from the identical reaction mixture). To date, MWs have been used in the phase-selective synthesis of porous materials, including inorganic porous materials AlPOs (aluminophosphates) [130,131,149-51], hybrid materials (Ni-glutarates such as MIL-77) [123,152-154], and MOFs (chromium-benzenedicarboxylates, aluminumbenzenetricarboxylates, see below) [124,155,156]. However, phase-selective synthesis with US irradiation has not been reported.
## 1.6 Crystal size reduction with MW/US
MW irradiation has been applied in the synthesis of small crystals, since it offers a fast and homogenous heating of the reaction mixture. The size of a material can generally be adjusted by controlling the nucleation/crystal growth rate, and smaller crystals are obtained when the nucleation rate is high [132,157-160]. MW affords uniform superheating with the creation of hot spots, which enhance the dissolution of reactants within a very short time and leads to the formation of nuclei in high concentration. Crystallization from the many nuclei leads to the rapid consumption of the reactant, and therefore the crystallization time should be small. Eventually, small particles are usually obtained and particle sizes are homogeneous. On the contrary, if a sufficient amount of time is allowed for crystallization by any means, crystal growth of larger particles will surely occur. Small particles will be also obtained in parallel because synthesis during a short time (which delays crystallization) may be also possible; therefore, the size will not be homogeneous.
Crystal size reduction with MW irradiation has been reported widely in the synthesis of nanoparticles of metals [62-64,161], metal oxides [62,63,161], zeolites [62,120,162], metal phosphates [62], MOFs [30,31,163] and various nanoporous materials [65,66].
The synthesis of nanostructured materials via US irradiation was summarized by Gedanken et al. [94]. In liquid precursors, sonochemical reactions occurred in a $\mathfrak{r}\sim200\:\mathrm{nm}$ ring surrounding the collapsing bubble, and therefore the products were nanocrystalline [90,94]. On the other hand, a high cooling rate, fast kinetics and short collapse of the bubbles did not allow the growth of the nuclei around the hot spots and ultimately hindered the organization and crystallization of the products. Therefore, in volatile precursors, amorphous nanoparticles are obtained through gas phase reactions. The size of the products also depends on the temperature gradient of the region where the reaction takes place. The temperature in this ring was lower than that inside of the collapsing bubble, but higher than the temperature of the bulk. In most US-assisted inorganic syntheses, the products vary in size, shape, structure, and in their solid phase (amorphous or crystalline), but they were always of nanometer size. Similar to MW irradiation, US also crystallizes fine particles of various inorganic [55,93,94,96] or MOF-type materials [30,48].
## 1.7 Purpose of this review
This review will mainly focus on the advantages of MW or US in the synthesis of porous materials such as MOFs. In particular, it will focus on the (i) phase-selective synthesis of MOFs with MW irradiation (origin and advantages), (ii) production of nano-sized or smaller crystals with MW or US, and (iii) quantitative degree of acceleration in rapid syntheses of MOFs with MW or US.
## 2. Discussion
## 2.1 Accelerated synthesis
The first MW synthesis of MOFs dealt with chromium-benzenetricarboxylate (named MIL-100-Cr) [125] and was synthesized in a reduced time at $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In that case, MW accelerated the reaction by 20 times compared with the conventional electric (CE) heating at the same temperature. The product yield and nitrogen adsorption capacity of MW synthesis for $4\mathrm{{h}}$ was comparable to CE synthesis in 4 d. The accelerated synthesis of MIL-100-Cr via MW was explained by the combination of the (i) fast dissolution of the precursors, especially metallic chromium, and (ii) acceleration of the condensation of the oxygen-metal networks. The superheating by MW irradiation gave rise to the fast dissolution of less soluble metal and organic precursors through the selective heating of dipolar species and hence the reduction of the nucleation period. A similar hypothesis was also proposed for the rapid synthesis of MIL101-Cr (chromium-benzenedicarboxylate) nanoparticles via MW irradiation [137], where the crystallization begins in the first minute of the reaction. The physicochemical and textural properties of MW-assisted synthesized MIL-101-Cr were similar to materials synthesized by using the conventional hydrothermal method and showed high and rapid adsorption of benzene. On the other hand, the synthesis of MIL-101-Cr with CE heating  takes several hours. The effect of MW irradiation time on the crystal growth of MOF-5 (zincbenzenedicarboxylate) was investigated by Ni et al. [164]. Very fast kinetics were observed in the synthesis of MOF-5, since the reaction only took 25 s with MW. However, long times (generally 7 – 24 h) in conventional hydrothermal synthesis have been reported with CE heating [165,166]. Interestingly, no crystal of MOF-5 was observed when the MW irradiation time was less than $20~\mathrm{s}$ ; however, the crystal growth process itself took only 5 s (meaning the synthesis was completed in 25 s). The drastic enhancement in the crystal growth via MW was due to the creation of hot spots by the superheating of the solvent and hence, the very fast
crystallization throughout the bulk solution. Instead, in the conventional solvothermal growth process, the crystal formation takes place near the walls or on dust particles, resulting in slow growth because there are very few seeds.
The rapid crystallization [145,146,167] of Cu-BTC (copper-benzenetricarboxylate) with increased product yield [145,146] was observed in the case of US irradiation. A highly efficient route to synthesize Cu-BTC by using US irradiation [146] with an addition of small amount of $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide was also demonstrated. The technique allows for a facile synthesis ( $81\%$ yield) with the shortest reaction time of 1 min at room temperature and ambient pressure, whereas other techniques such as MW and CE heating generally require longer reaction times with lesser product yields ( $68\%$ and $79\%$ , respectively). Because of the very short reaction time, it is not easy to differentiate nucleation or crystal growth stages with sonication. In addition, the Cu-BTC particles synthesized with US irradiation adsorbed comparatively higher amount of nitrogen than that of the materials synthesized by MW and CE heating (BET surface area of CE, MW and US-assisted synthesized Cu-BTC particles are 890, 1080 and $1156\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively).
Several comprehensive studies were reported regarding the synthesis of an ironbenzenedicarboxylate (named MIL-53-Fe) with US, MW, and CE heating at various reaction temperatures, and in all cases the rates of nucleation and crystal growth were $\mathrm{US}>\mathrm{MW}>>$ CE [168,169]. Haque et al. [168] explained the US- and MW-assisted acceleration in terms of the kinetic parameters of the MIL-53-Fe synthesis (Table 1). In order to understand the acceleration (nucleation or crystal growth) in more detail, two-step (changing heating methods from MW to CE or from CE to MW just after the nucleation is completed) syntheses were conducted. The crystallization curves of the two steps syntheses are shown in Figure 5. The activation energy $(E_{\mathrm{a}})$ and pre-exponential factors (A) of the syntheses decreased in the order of $\mathrm{US}>\mathrm{MW}>\mathrm{CE}$ for both the nucleation and crystal growth stages. The Arrhenius
equation, shown as equation 1, was used to obtain $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ and $A$ . The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ and $A$ were derived from the slope and intercept, respectively, of the logarithm of the Arrhenius equation (equation 2).
Arrhenius equation : k = Ae−Ea / RT (equation1)   
$\ln k=\ln A-{\frac{E_{a}}{R}}\cdot{\frac{1}{T}}{\mathrm{~(equation~}}2{\mathrm{)}}$   
where,   
$k$ : kinetic constant   
$A{\mathrm{:}}$ pre - exponential factor   
$E_{a}$ : activation energy   
$R$ : gas constant   
$T$ : absolute temperature
The reaction rates should be increased in the order of $\mathrm{US}<\mathrm{MW}<\mathrm{CE}$ if only the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ is considered and the $A$ values are the same in all cases. However, the overall rates decreased in the reverse order $(\mathrm{US}>\mathrm{MW}>>\mathrm{CE})$ ) and therefore, it was concluded that the increased reaction rates by US and MW, when compared with the synthesis by CE, were due to an increased $A$ rather than a decreased $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ . Moreover, MW irradiation for the synthesis of MIL53-Fe showed a stronger influence on the acceleration of crystal growth (55 times faster than CE heating) compared with nucleation (15.5 times faster than CE heating). Conversely, a large acceleration in the nucleation (6.9 times faster than CE heating), when compared with that in the crystal growth (1.7 times faster than CE heating), was observed in the MWassisted synthesis of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -BTC [170]. The acceleration in the nucleation stage by MW was due to the very large $A$ (about $1.4\mathrm{~x~}10^{10}$ times that of CE synthesis) in the Arrhenius plot. Gordon et al. [169] also studied the synthesis of MIL-53-Fe using the three heating methods (CE, MW, and US). Similar to Haque et al. [168], they also observed a huge acceleration in the US-assisted synthesis of MIL-53-Fe. Specifically,  around 30 and 128 times faster
crystallization was observed with MW and US, respectively, when compared with CE heating.
The syntheses of three analogous MOFs [171] (CPO-27s; composed of $\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Ni}$ and 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid) from very similar reaction compositions using three methods (US, MW, and CE) were also studied by Haque et al. [138]. The nucleation and crystal growth rates for the syntheses of CPO-27s were in the order of $\mathrm{US}>\mathrm{MW}>>\mathrm{CE}$ according to the heating method. Figure 6 shows the crystallization curves of CPO-27-Co; the synthesis with US was accelerated by 19 and 29 times for the nucleation and crystal growth, respectively, of CE synthesis. Compared with CE heating, the MW synthesis was also accelerated by about 10 times during nucleation and crystal growth. The authors demonstrated that the ‘acoustic cavitation’ and rapid heating/creation of hot spots with US and MW irradiation, respectively, were the driving forces for the accelerated syntheses.
The MW-assisted acceleration of the syntheses of analogous metalbenzenedicarboxylates (metal: Al, Cr and V) were analyzed with the Arrhenius and Eyring equations in an effort to further understand the accelerated syntheses with MW irradiation [139]. The physiochemical properties including nitrogen adsorption of the MOFs synthesized by MW irradiation were better than those of the MOFs obtained with CE heating. Irrespective of the type of metal ions $(\mathrm{Al}^{3+},\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{V}^{3+}$ ) in analogous MOFs [171], the acceleration degree $(r_{\mathrm{MW}}/r_{\mathrm{CE}})$ of nucleation and crystal growth were 4.9-32.7 and 20.5-35.9, respectively (see Table 2) [139]. MW heating afforded more favorable reaction coordinates (selective heating and changing the reaction profile), increased reaction sites, and/or increased reaction probability, and therefore, increased A. Acceleration degrees and $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ calculated with the Eyring equation were very similar to the results obtained with the Arrhenius equation. The activation free energies $(\Delta\mathrm{G}^{\ddagger})$ of MW syntheses were  lower than that of CE syntheses in every case. However, the activation enthalpy $(\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\ddagger})$ and activation entropy $(\Delta\mathbf{S}^{\dagger})$ values were higher, as shown in Table 2 and Figure 7. The activation enthalpy $(\varDelta H^{\neq})$ , activation entropy $(\varDelta\bar{S}^{\neq})$ , activation free energy $(\varDelta G^{\neq})$ and activation energy $(E_{a})$ were also calculated with the Eyring equation, presented as equation 3, for both nucleation and crystal growth. The kinetic constants of nucleation and crystal growth were plotted between $1/\mathrm{T}$ and $\ln(k/T)$ to follow the equation 5, and the activation enthalpy $(\varDelta H^{\neq})$ and activation entropy $(\varDelta\bar{S}^{\neq})$ were obtained from the slope and intercept, respectively. Activation free energy $(\varDelta G^{\neq})$ and activation energy $(E_{a})$ were calculated with equations of $\varDelta G^{\neq}=\varDelta H^{\neq}$ - $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{T}}\boldsymbol{\varDelta}\boldsymbol{S}^{\neq}$ and $E_{a}=\varDelta H^{\neq}+\mathrm{RT}$ , respectively.
The increased ${\Delta}{\bf S}^{\dagger}$ (or increased randomness) was stated to be the main reason for the decreased $\Delta\mathrm{G}^{\ddagger}$ values of the MW syntheses. Compliant with other results [168,170], it was stated that the accelerated syntheses via MW were mainly due to the increased $A$ values and decreased $\Delta\mathrm{G}^{\ddagger}$ of the Arrhenius equation and the Eyring equation, respectively.
Ahnfeldt et al. [172,173] synthesized two isoreticular aluminum containing MOFs, CAU-1- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ and CAU-1- $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , using 2-aminoterephthalic and 2,5-dihydroxyterpththalic acid, respectively, using MW and CE heating methods. The syntheses were investigated by in situ energy dispersive X-ray diffraction (EDXRD) experiments in the temperature range of $115{\mathrm{-}}145^{\mathrm{~\circ}}\mathrm{C}$ . In both cases, the MW irradiation led to faster nucleation and crystallization of the MOFs when compared with that with CE heating at all investigated reaction temperatures. Similar to other studies [139,168,170], the accelerated syntheses of CAU-1- $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ and CAU$\begin{array}{r}{{1}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}\end{array}$ with MW was also due to a large A. Nevertheless, the activation energy of MW synthesis was similar to that of CE synthesis. Even though the degree of acceleration was not calculated quantitatively, a very fast synthesis (within a few minutes) of chromiumbenzenedicarboxylates (MIL-53-Cr, MIL-101-Cr) with MW irradiation was reported whereas, the CE synthesis took days [124,126]. The analogous MOFs composed of lanthanides $(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+},\mathrm{Y}^{3+}$ , or $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ ) and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate were crystallized with huge acceleration by using US irradiation [148]. The BET surface areas and micropore volumes of the three MOFs obtained by the US method were higher than those of the samples obtained by CE heating.
Therefore, various porous materials, including MOFs, can be produced very quickly under MW or US, providing a continuous or cost-effective synthesis. Finally, US synthesis is much more efficient than MW synthesis in regard to the rapid synthesis of MOFs, and US also has the advantage of low operation temperatures and pressures. However, the versatility of US synthesis is quite limited compared with that of MW or CE synthesis.
## 2.2 Phase-selective synthesis of MOFs with MW
Generally, MW heating preferentially leads to the less stable phase in the synthesis of porous materials such as AlPOs [130,131,149,150] and zeolites [174], if any selectivity is observed. The conversion of a less stable phase into more stable phase with prolonged MW irradiation generates comparatively more stable structures [130,131,149,150]. In particular, an AlPO molecular sieve with a less stable pore structure (large pore size or low framework density) is preferentially obtained with MW heating, due to decreased synthesis time. For example, VPI-5 molecular sieves (having 18-membered rings; 18-MR) and AFI (having 12-
MR) molecular sieves are selectively synthesized rather than AFI (12-MR) or AEL (10-MR) and CHA (8-MR) structures obtained in CE synthesis, respectively, in reduced amounts of time under MW heating [131,150].
Similar to the synthesis of AlPOs [130,131,149,150] and zeolites [174], MW heating is selective in the synthesis of less stable phases of porous MOFs [124,155].  Less stable MOF phases are obtained at the beginning of the reactions, and the comparatively more stable phase replaces the former phase as the irradiation time is increased [124,155]. The crystallization of two highly porous chromium-benzenedicarboxylates (MIL-53-Cr and MIL101-Cr) by CE and MW heating has been investigated in order to understand the phaseselectivity and phase-transitions of these solids [124]. Under certain reaction conditions, with CE heating, kinetically favorable MIL-101-Cr (lower-density phase) was obtained during the early stage of the reaction, while the thermodynamically favorable MIL-53-Cr phase (higherdensity phase) was obtained at the expense of MIL-101-Cr with longer reaction times.
However, under similar conditions, MW heating provided the selective synthesis of MIL-101-Cr with comparatively higher yield by preventing the conversion into the thermodynamically favorable phase (MIL-53-Cr) (Figure 8). The selective formation of the kinetically favored MIL-101-Cr phase was mainly ascribed to the very fast kinetics of nucleation and crystallization under MW heating [124]. Moreover, three porous aluminumbenzenetricarboxylates (MIL-100, MIL-110 and MIL-96) were synthesized in a consecutive manner from the same reaction compositions by changing the heating methods (CE and MW heating) and reaction time [155].  With the increase of reaction time, the least stable phase (MIL-100) converts into two consecutive phases (MIL-110 and the most stable MIL-96, respectively). The inter-conversion of a less stable phase into a more stable phase was carried out via recombination of the degraded precursors. In that study, MW heating was selective toward the crystallization of the less stable MIL-100 and MIL-110 phases; whereas CE
heating ultimately produced the most stable MIL-96 phase. The rapid heating and fast crystallization with MW irradiation likely led to the selective synthesis of the less stable and kinetically favored MIL-100 or MIL-110 phases. Therefore, MW heating can serve as a phase-selective synthetic method for less stable or kinetically controlled phases in the preparation of MOFs from the same reactant mixtures at the same reaction temperatures (Figure 9).
Hybrid complexes of different phases from identical reaction mixtures of Co(II) and cis-4-cyclohexene-1-2-dicarboxylic acid were synthesized at different reaction temperatures by Seo et al. [153]. The pure cis conformation of cyclohexene-dicarboxylate was selectively obtained via MW heating within very short time $(\sim5\mathrm{{min})}$ at a low temperature. Notably, CE heating required $48\mathrm{{h}}$ and hence MW heating is a rapid and clean method to synthesize hightemperature phases in high purities at relatively low temperatures. Delgado and Zamora reported [175] a coordination network, which could only be isolated using MW irradiation from $\mathrm{Cu(HCO_{2})_{2}{\cdot}x H_{2}O}$ and 2,2-dipyridyldisulfide. The reaction proceeded through the partial oxidation of 2,2-dipyridyldisulfide to sulfate over MW heating and gave rise to three different coordination complexes having $\mathrm{{Cu(I)}}$ , $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{I}/\operatorname{II})$ and $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ as the central metal ions. In another work, Maniam and co-workers observed that $\mathrm{Ni_{2}(B D C)_{2}(D A B C O)]{\cdot}(D M F)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{4}}$ , one of the polymorphs of the pillared-layered $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})$ framework, could be selectively synthesized due to the rapid nucleation attainable with MW irradiation [176].
Liang et al. [156] studied the synthesis of three zirconium oxide-based MOFs (MIL140A, MIL-140B, and MIL-140A- $\mathrm{.NH}_{2}$ with 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate, 2,6- naphthalenedicarboxylate and 2-amino-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate linkers, respectively) under MW and CE heating. In that study, MW heating produced comparatively more crystalline MIL-140A in efficient way. On the other hand, along with MIL-140A, CE heating led to the formation of another MOF (UiO-66 or zirconium-benzenedicarboxylate) as an impurity. The rapid ramping rate in the MW-assisted synthesis of MIL-140A was stated to be the key to the improved phase purity and crystallinity. Moreover, MIL-140A- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ (analogous to MIL140A) was selectively isolated with MW irradiation; however the MOF could not be attained with CE heating. Interestingly, even with changing the reaction parameters (like reaction time, reactant concentration, reactant composition) in CE heating, only UiO-66-NH2 could be obtained rather than MIL-140A- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ .
Jhung et al. reported [123] the hydrothermal synthesis of two porous MOFs (nickel glutarates) with very similar physicochemical properties under various conditions by MW irradiation and CE heating. Cubic nickel glutarate, $\mathrm{[Ni_{20}(C_{5}H_{6}O_{4})_{20}(H_{2}O)_{8}]\cdot40H_{2}O}$ , was synthesized by CE heating in several hours or days. However, MW heating resulted in the similar phase (cubic) during the early stage of the reaction, and finally redissolved to recrystallize a new more stable phase of nickel glutarate
$\mathrm{[Ni_{22}(C_{5}H_{6}O_{4})_{20}(O H)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{10}]\cdot38H_{2}O]}$ with a tetragonal structure. Therefore, the authors attributed the preferential formation of the tetragonal phase under MW irradiation to the extremely fast $(>2,000$ times compared with CE heating) crystallization (due to superheating or creation of hot spots in the reaction mixture), in which formation of the less-favorable cubic phase was merely a transient and intermediate step.
Therefore, new (especially metastable or less stable) MOF phases, which are difficult to synthesize or are not yet known may be obtained by utilizing MW irradiation. Even though there is no report of phase-selective synthesis using US, the possibility exists because of the similarities between US and MW (in terms of accelerated synthesis and crystal size reduction, see below).
## 2.3 Crystal size reduction
The shape and size of porous materials are very important in various applications including membranes, imaging contrast agents, catalysis, separations and drug delivery [25,157-160,177-184]. Several advantages of using small crystals reported in the field of catalysis and diffusion include following: (i) catalysis is more effective, (ii) coke deposition is less severe, (iii) diffusion limitations are less severe, (iv) template extraction and cation exchange are easier, and (v) regeneration of used catalyst is less difficult for small crystals [157,160]. Nanoparticles of MOFs can be used for various purposes such as drug delivery and membrane. Therefore, reproducible syntheses of nanosized MOFs are important. Nanosized MOFs are typically obtained by the proper optimization of conventional reaction conditions; however, this is not very efficient considering the feasibility, homogeneity, and production cost of the synthesis. To synthesize uniform nanocrystals, a single nucleation event should be ensured and additional nucleation during the successive crystal growth process should be prevented [185]. Reaction temperature and heating method strongly influence the nucleation or crystal growth steps, and therefore, determine the size and shape of the final product. Thermal gradients in the reaction mixture (in CE synthesis) cause poor nucleation and inhomogeneities in the growth process and as a consequence, broad size distributions are observed.
Ni et al. [164] reported the synthesis of small MOF-5 crystals from zinc and 1,4- benzenedicarboxylic acid using MW irradiation. Small particles of MOF-5 could be obtained by MW heating with reduced concentrations of the reaction mixture. Submicrometer scale MOF-5 crystals were observed with MW heating, whereas, normal CE heating  yields micrometer scale crystals [186]. The MW-assisted synthesis of highly porous MIL-101-Cr was reported for the first time by Jhung et al. [137]. In that study, very fast crystallization due to MW heating resulted in spherical nanoparticles during the early stage of crystallization that ultimately became cubic in shape with longer reaction times. The crystals obtained with MW irradiation were $40{-}80~\mathrm{nm}$ in size, whereas crystals on the micrometer scale were obtained with CE heating.
Liu et al. [37] stated that the homogeneous heating of MW creates a uniform seeding condition where many seeds form in an extremely short period of time, which ultimately hinders the seeds growth. They synthesized cobalt(II)- and manganese(II)-2,6- naphthalenedicarboxylates via MW irradiation. MW-assisted solvothermal synthesis produced $5{\cdot}20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ -sized crystals with a narrow size distribution and relatively uniform shape, whereas, CE synthesis gave rise to the formation of $50{-}200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ -sized crystals. A similar explanation was attributed to the synthesis of Cu-BTC crystals via MW irradiation, which led to crystals with much smaller dimensions compared with those produced by conventional heating [187].
Nano-sized Cu-BTC crystals $(10–40\mathrm{nm})$ were obtained in 5 min under US, and were much smaller than those synthesized using the conventional solvothermal method $(10-30~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ [98]. The synthesis of Cu-BTC with three different heating methods (US, MW, and CE) produces nano-sized crystals in a short reaction time [146]. In that study, the US-assisted CuBTC particles $200\mathrm{nm}$ ) were 50 and 100 times smaller in size than those obtained with MW $(10\upmu\mathrm{m})$ and CE heating $(20~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ , respectively.
Comprehensive studies regarding the synthesis of MIL-53-Fe with US, MW, and CE heating methods were conducted by Haque et al. [168] and Gordon et al. [169]. The effects of the heating techniques on the crystal morphologies of MIL-53-Fe crystals were similar in both studies. The crystal sizes obtained in those studies followed the order of $\mathrm{CE>>MW>}$ US (Figure 10) in terms of heating method. The trend of the particle size remained unchanged in the synthesis of CPO-27-Co [138]. The physiochemical properties of the MOFs obtained with three different heating methods were comparable and the MOFs synthesized with US irradiation (having the smallest particle size) showed the highest porosity or nitrogen
adsorption capacity compared with the CPO-27-Co MOFs synthesized by MW and CE heating. The rapid and homogeneous heating by US and MW irradiation gave rise to the superheating of the reaction precursor, which resulted in homogeneous nucleation and small crystal formation. In the case of CE heating, the necessary time to reach the reaction temperature was very long and led to multi-step nucleation and allowed for simultaneous crystal growth. Hence, the particle size may be not small enough or homogeneous.
Jung et al. [129] synthesized MOF-177, formulated as $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O(B T B)}_{2}\:(\mathrm{BTB}=4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}-$ benzene-1,3,5-triyltribenzoate) with three different heating methods (US, MW, and CE heating). According to the SEM images, the US- and MW-assisted synthesized MOF-177s had particle with sizes ${5}{-}20~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and $15.50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , and were about 30 times smaller than MOF177 particles synthesized conventionally and observed with an optical microscope. The highly accelerated nucleation in US-assisted synthesis quickly depletes the nutrient for the crystal growth in the bulk mixture and results in uniform and fine crystals with significantly higher yield and higher nitrogen adsorption properties compared with those of MW and CEassisted syntheses.
MOF-5 microcrystals $(5-15~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ ) were synthesized by Sabouni et al. [188] by applying MW irradiation after a pre-treatment by US. This combined two-step technique resulted in smaller and more regularly shaped crystals; the crystal size was reduced by a magnitude of approximately 10 when compared with those synthesized by CE heating.
The synthesis of nanoscale porous iron-fumarate (MIL-88A) was conducted using various synthesis methods [127]. The optimized reaction conditions produced relatively large crystals under CE heating ( $250\mathrm{nm}$ ), whereas around $100\mathrm{nm}$ sized-crystals were produced in the US-assisted synthesis and the finest particles $<100\mathrm{nm}$ ) were obtained by MW irradiation. More specifically, MW irradiation (600W) at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 min produced nanoparticles of around $20\mathrm{nm}$ .
The facile synthesis of nano-sized MIL-101-Cr under optimized reaction conditions such as pH and water concentration by utilizing MW irradiation was demonstrated [128]. MIL-101-Cr crystals, around $50\mathrm{nm}$ in size, were produced in comparable yield $(37\%)$ by MW irradiation in 15 min from diluted reactant mixtures containing a base. Under very similar conditions, CE heating produces around $100\mathrm{nm}$ sized crystals ( $39\%$ yield) in $24\mathrm{h}$ .
US wave was employed to synthesize small particles of ZIF-8 (zeolitic imidazolate framework) from zinc nitrate and 2-methylimidazole [147]. The nano-sized ZIF-8 particles obtained (around $700\mathrm{nm}$ ) were approximately 400 times smaller than conventionally synthesized particles $(250\upmu\mathrm{m})$ .
Very recently, Vilela et al. [189] synthesized nanosized analogous MOFs assembled from bisphosphonates and lanthanides (Eu, Gd and Tb) using US, MW, and CE heating. One of the synthesized MOFs [Tb(Hpmd) $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ with a particle size of 100-150  crystallizes with MW irradiation in only 5 s. The size of the [Eu(Hpmd) $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O)}\dot{]}_{\ast}$ ] MOF produced in US-assisted synthesis was also on the nanometer scale.
There are various available reports that exemplify the reduced size of MOFs under MW or US, and they are summarized in Table 3. Even though there was only one exemption [127], the size decreased in the order of CE $>$ MW $\mathrm{>}$ US. In general, nanoparticles were produced with MW and especially with US due to the homogeneous reaction and short reaction time. Therefore, US synthesis is highly desirable to produce small MOFs even though the synthesis is not very widely used so far.
## 3. Concluding remarks and perspectives
In this review we compiled the advantages of non-conventional heating methods, namely, MW and US irradiation, for the synthesis of MOF materials. The superiority of MW or US over CE heating was pointed out in terms of the degree of acceleration on the reaction rate, the selective synthesis of some MOF structures and finally, the size reduction of MOF crystals. MW and US heating techniques represent capable ways to achieve short reaction times and small crystals, both of which are crucial for the applications of MOFs. Recently, both techniques are very effective in the rapid and facile synthesis of MOFs even in seconds, and ensured environmental friendliness, low consumption of energy, and a low production cost and potential viability in continuous methods. After reviewing the studies on accelerated synthesis of MOFs with MW or US irradiation, it can be stated that the reaction rate and crystal size follow the orders of CE<<MW<US and CE $>$ MW $\mathrm{\Delta>}$ US, respectively, when the three heating methods are employed separately to synthesize particular MOFs. The size of the MOF crystals obtained with MW or US irradiation was decreased drastically (up to nanometer scale), and therefore it is obvious that these alternative heating methods will ultimately impose a positive effect on various applications (related to nano-materials) such as thin films, columns, electronic devices, and drug delivery among others. Notably, it has also been confirmed that MW irradiation provides some unusual crystal structures with excellent phase purities that are hard or impossible to obtain via conventional methods.
Even though the MW- or US-assisted syntheses of MOFs are typically performed on a laboratory scale, it is expected that the exceptional advantages over conventional method will facilitate the scale up for industrial use in near future. From this review the high efficiency of MW and US when compared with CE heating in MOF synthesis is undoubtedly understandable; however, detailed studies and future research are expected as follows:
(i) US or MW irradiation with distinct characteristics shows impressive outcomes in various aspects of MOF synthesis and each method has some advantages in the synthesis. The utilization of the combined methods in a simultaneous or consecutive fashion might result in a new strategy to synthesize MOFs more efficiently. The concept of combined
US/MW (excluding pretreatment with US) has been applied in the field of organic synthesis [190,191].
(ii) Generally, the solvothermal synthesis of MOFs is performed under high temperatures and pressures (autogenous pressure in a sealed reactor), and therefore, both CE and MW heating were quite successful as the reaction mixtures (in sealed reactors used for CE or MW synthesis) generate high pressure autogenously when heated. Synthesis of MOFs with US irradiation under high temperatures and autogenous pressures has not been reported, and hence, US-assisted synthesis of MOFs is not common because high temperature was generally required in MOF synthesis. Therefore, it can be anticipated that under high temperatures and pressures, US irradiation would produce MOFs that cannot be produced under ambient conditions in the shortest reaction time, even though US synthesis is not very common at high pressures.
(iii) The phase-selective or preferential synthesis of MOFs with MW irradiation in terms of the relative stability (with reaction time) of the phases has not been studied extensively. More detailed experimental and theoretical studies with proper explanations will not only broaden the applicability of this technique particularly in the synthesis of less stable or metastable (or highly porous) MOFs but also in the synthesis of novel MOFs.
(iv) Layer-structured MOFs or nanofilm of MOFs are recently very promising for creating future nanotechnological devices such as electrodes for fuel cells, sensors, catalytic devices and so on [192,193]. Since, MW and US heating methods have been reported for the production of conventional MOF particles (especially with small size) in very short irradiation time, the fabrication of nanofilms or nano-architechtured layered sheets of MOF by using US or MW techniques may also be meaningful.
(v) Even though MW and US heating are superior to conventional heating in various aspects including the synthesis of MOF, AlPOs, and ZIFs among others, the synthesis of
many porous solids have not been reported via those methods. Therefore, the potentials of MW or US irradiation can also be employed in the synthesis of porous materials like covalent organic frameworks (COFs) [194], covalent triazine-based frameworks (CTFs) [195,196], and covalent organic polymers (COPs) [197] among others.
